<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">El Mirador</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 150 CE (approx.) · capital El Mirador</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The greatest Preclassic Maya city, in the northern Petén forest, with some of the largest pyramids ever built and causeways linking it to neighbouring centres.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlement at El Mirador expanded from about 600 BCE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirador-basin">Mirador Basin</a> of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Fertile swamp soils, carried up to build raised gardens, supported a population large enough for monumental building on a scale unmatched in later Maya history.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The La <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/danta-state">Danta</a> and El <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tigray">Tigre</a> complexes are among the largest structures of the ancient world by volume. Stucco masks of deities decorated platform façades, and raised causeways (sakbeob) linked El Mirador with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakbe">Nakbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-tintal">Tintal</a> and other sites, suggesting a regional state. Some scholars connect it with the later snake-head emblem of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a>, but this remains debated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>El Mirador was largely abandoned around 150 CE, possibly through environmental degradation, drought or political collapse, a crisis shared across the basin. A smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic</a> reoccupation followed. Its rediscovery showed that Maya civilisation reached great complexity centuries earlier than once thought.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/el-mirador.jpg" alt="El Mirador" width="320" height="375"><figcaption>Image: Konjiki1 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pir%C3%A1mide_de_la_Danta,_Pet%C3%A9n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>El Mirador</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kan kingdom (Preclassic)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-225&amp;focus=el-mirador" aria-label="El Mirador on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2239 1519 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where El Mirador lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2559 1775h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2559 1775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-225&amp;focus=el-mirador">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

